Dataset ecommerce_rlp_oneway {
  label: 'ecommerce (docs RLP example) - forced rlp_propagation one_way'
  description: '''
    The docs example again, identical to the `ecommerce` dataset except for ONE character-level change:

      relationship(fact_orders_test.product_id > dim_products_test.id, true, 'one_way', rlp_propagation='one_way')

    That is the "blocking relationship" the doc names. Everything else is byte-identical to `ecommerce`.

    WHY. Per Khai, omitting rlp_propagation gives 'two_way', so the doc's example cannot error as written.
    Forcing 'one_way' on that one edge is therefore the ONLY configuration in which the documented error can
    appear. If it still does not error here, then the documented error condition does not exist on this
    workspace at all, and the customer's error must come from something else entirely - at which point the
    right move is Christo's actual dataset code, not more guessing on our side.

    THE TEST, in production as an Explorer/Viewer, with the test user's `region` attribute set to 'North':

      1. Total Orders, no breakdown.
           2 -> RLP enforced. Baseline good.
           4 -> not enforced. 0 -> region value does not match 'North'.

      2. Total Orders broken down by Dim Products Test -> Name, with NO city field:

           explore {
             dimensions { dim_products_test.name }
             measures   { total_orders }
           }

         This is the doc's failing query, on the doc's graph, with the doc's own prescribed blocking
         relationship forced to one_way. It is the strongest possible test.

           Errors   -> the error condition is real and needs an explicit rlp_propagation='one_way'. Then
                       Christo must have that set somewhere in his real dataset, and that is the answer to
                       CF-14071.
           No error -> the error cannot be produced on this workspace in any configuration we can construct.
                       Escalate: either it is version/flag dependent, or the trigger is something not in the
                       docs at all.

      3. For completeness: Total Qty from Fact Inventory (no path to dim_cities whatsoever). Expect 110.
  '''
  data_source_name: 'demodb'

  models: [
    dim_users, dim_products_test, dim_cities,
    dim_merchants, dim_categories,
    fact_orders_test, fact_inventory
  ]

  relationships: [
    relationship(fact_orders_test.user_id > dim_users.id, true, 'one_way'),

    // The doc's "blocking relationship", with RLP propagation forced one_way.
    relationship(fact_orders_test.product_id > dim_products_test.id, true, 'one_way', rlp_propagation='one_way'),

    relationship(fact_inventory.product_id > dim_products_test.id, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(dim_users.city_id > dim_cities.id, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(dim_products_test.merchant_id > dim_merchants.id, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(dim_products_test.category_id > dim_categories.id, true, 'one_way')
  ]

  metric total_orders {
    label: 'Total Orders'
    type: 'number'
    definition: @aql count(fact_orders_test.id);;
  }

  permission regional_access {
    field: r(dim_cities.region)
    operator: 'matches_user_attribute'
    value: 'region' // user attribute
  }
}
